===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|ānʹjəl}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈeɪn.d͡ʒəl/}}
* {{lb|en|Caribbean|or|poetic|nocat=1}} {{IPA|en|/ˈeɪn.d͡ʒɛl/}}
* {{a|dialectal, obsolete}} {{enPR|ănʹjəl}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈæn.d͡ʒəl/}}<ref>{{R:Bingham IP|74}}</ref>
* {{audio|en|en-uk-angel.ogg|Audio (UK)}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-angel.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪndʒəl|s=2}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|angel}}, {{m|enm|aungel}}, {{m|enm|ængel}}, {{m|enm|engel}}, from {{inh|en|ang|anġel}}, {{m|ang|ænġel}}, {{m|ang|enġel}}, {{m|ang|enċġel|t=angel, messenger}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*angil}}, borrowed from {{der|en|la|angelus}}, itself from {{der|en|grc|ἄγγελος|t=messenger}}; and also in part from {{der|en|xno|angele}}, {{m|fro|angle}}, from the same Latin source. The religious sense of the Greek word first appeared in the [[Septuagint]] as a translation of the Hebrew word {{m|he|מַלְאָךְ|tr=malʾāḵ|t=messenger}} or {{m|he|[[יהוה]] מַלְאָךְ|tr=malʾāḵ YHWH|t=messenger of [[YHWH]]}}.

Use of the term in some churches to refer to a church official derives from interpreting the "angels" of the {{w|Seven churches of Asia}} in ''[[Revelation]]'' as being bishops or ministers rather than angelic beings.

